  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nAfter a completed `CURLOPT_CONNECT_ONLY` transfer, detaching the shared connection pool via `curl_easy_setopt(easy, CURLOPT_SHARE, NULL)` causes `curl_easy_send()` / `curl_easy_recv()` to silently operate on an unrelated connection from a different pool. Data intended for server B is sent to server A, and A's response is accepted as B's.\n\nTested against commit `c2676bf9e639e31e44b12a2cc82cf2046231b056`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`state.lastconnect_id` stores only a bare numeric connection ID (`lib/urldata.h:529`). Connection IDs are pool-local counters (`lib/conncache.c:576`), so independent pools both assign ID 0 to their first connection.\n\n`Curl_getconnectinfo()` resolves `lastconnect_id` via `Curl_cpool_get_conn()`, which picks the pool from the easy handle's **current** share/multi state (`lib/conncache.c:256-266`), then matches only `connection_id` (`lib/conncache.c:835`).\n\n`setopt_share()` accepts the detach because `data->conn` is NULL after `CONNECT_ONLY` (`lib/setopt.c:1423-1428`), but neither `setopt_share()` nor `Curl_share_easy_unlink()` invalidates `lastconnect_id`. The raw I/O helper `easy_connection()` only checks that `CURLOPT_CONNECT_ONLY` is enabled, not that the resolved connection is the one that was created (`lib/easy.c:1219-1241`).\n\nResult: the retained ID 0 is resolved in the wrong pool, returning an unrelated connection.\n\n## Relation to CVE-2020-8231\n\nThis is an incomplete-fix variant. CVE-2020-8231's fix replaced a stale connection pointer with a pool-local numeric ID, but the ID is not globally unique. The post-`CONNECT_ONLY` share-detach transition was not addressed. The underlying pool-local-ID collision was noted in [HackerOne #948876](https://hackerone.com/reports/948876) and [PR #8524](https://github.com/curl/curl/pull/8524).\n\n## Reproduction\n### Steps\n\nFrom the curl source root, place the three files above under `poc/` and run:\n\n```sh\ndocker build -t curl-33-poc -f poc/Dockerfile .\ndocker run --rm curl-33-poc\n```\n\n### Expected output\n\n```\nSEED_RESULT=0\nSHARE_CONNECT_RESULT=0\nCONNECT_ONLY_RESULT=0\nBEFORE_GETINFO=0\nBEFORE_PORT=18081\nSHARE_DETACH_RESULT=0\nAFTER_GETINFO=0\nAFTER_PORT=18080\nSEND_RESULT=0\nSENT=20\nRECV_RESULT=0\nRECV=A_REPLY\nSERVER_A_MARKER=YES\nSERVER_B_MARKER=NO\nVERDICT=VULNERABLE\n```\n\nKey observations:\n\n- `BEFORE_PORT=18081`: active socket points to B right after `CONNECT_ONLY`.\n- `AFTER_PORT=18080`: after `CURLOPT_SHARE=NULL`, active socket silently switches to A.\n- `SERVER_A_MARKER=YES`: A received `SECRET_FOR_SERVER_B`.\n- `SERVER_B_MARKER=NO`: B received nothing.\n- `RECV=A_REPLY`: `curl_easy_recv()` returned A's response.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker controlling server A receives bytes the application intended for server B and can supply bytes accepted as B's response. This is a cross-peer confidentiality and integrity violation.\n\nPractical severity is **Low**: the attacker cannot force the required API lifecycle. The application must independently (1) keep an attacker-controlled connection alive in the private pool, (2) use a shared connection pool with `CONNECT_ONLY`, (3) detach the share after the transfer, and (4) then use raw I/O on the handle.",
